{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Patients diagnosed before age 50 exhibit higher tumor proliferation activity (measured by MKI67, CCNB1, MCM2 expression) than patients diagnosed at or after age 50.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether younger patients (<50 years) have elevated proliferation signature compared to older patients (≥50 years).",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_gene_expression.csv.  2. Strip version suffixes from Ensembl IDs in the expression index.  3. Map MKI67, CCNB1, MCM2 HGNC symbols to their Ensembl IDs.  4. Extract those three genes’ expression, transpose to patient‐level, and compute a proliferation signature (mean expression).  5. From clinical data, define two age groups: <50 and ≥50 years at diagnosis.  6. Merge signature score with age group by patient barcode, exclude missing values.  7. Compare signature distributions between age groups using a two‐sided Mann–Whitney U test.  8. Generate a boxplot of proliferation signature by age group with U statistic and p‐value annotated.",  
      "deliverables":"• Table with patient barcodes, age group label, and proliferation signature.  • Mann–Whitney U test result (U statistic and p‐value).  • Boxplot showing signature distributions for <50 vs ≥50 groups with statistical annotation."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"African American patients have a higher tumor mutational burden (TMB) than White patients in the TCGA-BRCA cohort.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess whether race (African American vs White) is associated with differences in TMB.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_mutations.csv.  2. Compute per‐patient TMB by counting mutation records in cleaned_mutations.csv.  3. From clinical data, filter patients with race == \"BLACK OR AFRICAN AMERICAN\" or race == \"WHITE\", exclude others/missing.  4. Merge TMB with race labels by patient barcode.  5. Perform a two‐sided Mann–Whitney U test comparing TMB between the two racial groups.  6. Create a violin or boxplot of TMB for each race with test annotation.",  
      "deliverables":"• Data frame with patient barcodes, race label, and TMB.  • Mann–Whitney U test result (U statistic and p‐value).  • Violin or boxplot comparing TMB distributions for African American vs White patients with annotation."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"A higher immune infiltration signature (mean expression of CD8A, CD3D, GZMB) is associated with longer time to new tumor event (disease‐free interval).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether patients with stronger immune signatures experience prolonged disease‐free survival (days_to_new_tumor).",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_gene_expression.csv.  2. Strip Ensembl version suffixes in the expression index.  3. Map CD8A, CD3D, GZMB to their Ensembl IDs and extract their expression, transpose to patient‐level.  4. Compute an immune signature per patient as the mean of these three genes.  5. From clinical data, extract days_to_new_tumor for patients with new_tumor_event == 'YES'.  6. Merge immune signature with days_to_new_tumor by barcode, exclude missing.  7. Compute Spearman correlation between immune signature and days_to_new_tumor and report rho and p‐value.  8. Plot a scatterplot with regression line (or lowess) of signature vs days_to_new_tumor with annotation of correlation.",  
      "deliverables":"• Table of patient barcodes, immune signature score, and days_to_new_tumor.  • Spearman correlation coefficient (rho) and p‐value.  • Scatterplot with regression line and correlation annotation."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Patients with a history of prior malignancy exhibit a higher proportion of high‐impact (IMPACT == 'HIGH') somatic mutations compared to patients without prior malignancy.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Determine whether prior malignancy status correlates with the functional severity of mutations.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_mutations.csv.  2. From clinical data, label patients as prior_malignancy == 'Yes' or 'No'.  3. For each patient in the mutation dataset, calculate the proportion of mutations where IMPACT == 'HIGH'.  4. Merge proportion_high_impact with prior_malignancy label by barcode.  5. Compare distributions of high‐impact mutation proportion using a two‐sided Mann–Whitney U test.  6. Create a boxplot or violin plot of proportion_high_impact stratified by prior malignancy status with test annotation.",  
      "deliverables":"• Data frame with patient barcodes, prior_malignancy label, and proportion of HIGH‐impact mutations.  • Mann–Whitney U test result (U statistic and p‐value).  • Boxplot/violin plot comparing high‐impact mutation proportions by prior malignancy status."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Patients harboring any TP53 mutation have elevated tumor proliferation signature (mean of MKI67 and CCNE1 expression) compared to patients without TP53 mutations.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test if TP53‐mutant tumors show increased proliferation gene expression.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_mutations.csv and cleaned_gene_expression.csv.  2. Strip Ensembl version suffixes in expression index.  3. Map MKI67 and CCNE1 to Ensembl IDs, extract their expression, and compute a proliferation signature per patient.  4. Identify patients with ≥1 TP53 mutation in cleaned_mutations.csv.  5. Label patients as TP53_mutant or TP53_wildtype.  6. Merge proliferation signature with mutation‐status label by barcode, exclude missing.  7. Compare signature between mutant vs wildtype using Mann–Whitney U test.  8. Plot boxplot of proliferation signature by TP53 status with test annotation.",  
      "deliverables":"• Table with patient barcodes, TP53 mutation status, and proliferation signature.  • Mann–Whitney U test result (U statistic and p‐value).  • Boxplot showing proliferation signature by TP53 mutation status."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"HER2 IHC‐equivocal patients exhibit intermediate ERBB2 (HER2) gene expression compared to HER2‐negative and HER2‐positive groups.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess ERBB2 expression differences across her2_ihc_status categories: Negative, Equivocal, Positive.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_gene_expression.csv.  2. Strip Ensembl version suffixes in expression index.  3. Map ERBB2 to its Ensembl ID and extract its expression per patient.  4. From clinical data, select patients with non‐missing her2_ihc_status in {Negative, Equivocal, Positive}.  5. Merge ERBB2 expression with her2_ihc_status by barcode.  6. Perform a Kruskal‐Wallis test across the three groups.  7. Create a boxplot of ERBB2 expression by her2_ihc_status with group statistics and post‐hoc pairwise comparisons if appropriate.",  
      "deliverables":"• Data frame of patient barcodes, her2_ihc_status, and ERBB2 expression.  • Kruskal‐Wallis test result (H statistic and p‐value) and post‐hoc pairwise p‐values.  • Boxplot of ERBB2 expression across the three IHC status groups with annotations."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Advanced pathologic stage (Stage III) is associated with higher expression of the extracellular matrix gene MMP9 compared to early stage (Stage I) tumors.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Evaluate whether tumor stage correlates with elevated MMP9 expression.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_gene_expression.csv.  2. Strip version suffixes in Ensembl IDs.  3. Map MMP9 to its Ensembl ID and extract its expression per patient.  4. From clinical data, select patients with pathologic_stage in {\"Stage I\",\"Stage IA\",\"Stage IIIA\",\"Stage IIIB\",…}.  5. Define two groups: Stage I (combine Stage I and IA) vs Stage III (combine IIIA, IIIB, IIIC).  6. Merge MMP9 expression with stage group by barcode.  7. Compare expression between groups via Mann–Whitney U test.  8. Plot boxplot of MMP9 expression by stage group with annotation.",  
      "deliverables":"• Data frame of patient barcodes, stage group, and MMP9 expression.  • Mann–Whitney U test result (U statistic and p‐value).  • Boxplot comparing MMP9 levels in Stage I vs Stage III tumors."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Patients who received radiation therapy display higher expression of immune checkpoint genes PDCD1 and CD274 (PD‐L1) than patients who did not receive radiation.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether radiation therapy is associated with upregulation of PDCD1 and CD274 expression.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_gene_expression.csv.  2. Strip Ensembl version suffixes in expression index.  3. Map PDCD1 and CD274 to their Ensembl IDs and extract their expression.  4. From clinical data, label patients by radiation_therapy == 'YES' or 'NO', exclude missing.  5. Merge gene expression with radiation label by barcode.  6. Compute for each patient an immune checkpoint signature (mean of PDCD1 and CD274).  7. Compare signatures between radiated vs non‐radiated patients via Mann–Whitney U test.  8. Generate a boxplot of immune checkpoint signature by radiation status with statistical annotation.",  
      "deliverables":"• Table with patient barcodes, radiation status, and PDCD1/CD274 signature.  • Mann–Whitney U test result (U statistic and p‐value).  • Boxplot of immune checkpoint signature for YES vs NO radiation groups."  
    }  
  }  
]}
